,"offense":"The Colts offense has struggled, ranking 25th or worse in three of the five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix has stayed close to what game situations call for. Daniel Jones has run the passing game, with Tyler Warren leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Colts defense has been well below average by EPA allowed per play so far. Its strongest area has been success rate allowed, where it ranks 11th, and its weakest has been takeaways per game, where it ranks 30th. Laiatu Latu has been as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise is Adetomiwa Adebawore, who has 5 sacks despite playing only 53% of the snaps. In terms of scheme, they have the 2nd lowest blitz rate in the league.","special":"The Colts have been about average in how often they go for it on fourth down. Their kickers have made 86% of field goals, which is about average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the secondary, about 29% of the money on the books. Their tight ends were among the oldest in the league.","players":"Daniel Jones has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 1,974 yards. Targets have been spread around, with Tyler Warren and Keenan Allen leading a group where nobody has more than a fifth of the looks. Jonathan Taylor has handled most of the work in the backfield with 71% of the carries. On defense, Adetomiwa Adebawore leads the team with 5 sacks.","coaching":"Shane Steichen is calling the offense as head coach and Lou Anarumo is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2023 and 2025 Packers, both under Matt LaFleur. Their defense looks most like the 2025 Packers and the 2022 Saints under Dennis Allen.","trends":"The offense went from 9th in EPA per play in 2025 to 31st so far this season, a big step back. The defense moved from 16th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 23rd so far this season, a modest decline. The biggest change in approach was two point attempt rate, which went from 13% to 0%.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}